 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "ROTHERA. The British Antarctic Survey's main base, on Adelaide Island, opened in 1975 and named after John Rothera, a surveyor who worked in the area. In the southern summer more than a hundred people live here, in winter about twenty. It's the biggest British base on the continent, and runs on gravel, aviation fuel and a great deal of tea.",
   "plaque": "antarctic_rothera",
   "title": "Rothera Research Station"
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "THE RRS SIR DAVID ATTENBOROUGH. Britain's polar research ship, in service since 2020, ploughs to Rothera each summer with supplies. A public vote to name her picked 'Boaty McBoatface', so the name went to a small yellow robot submarine on board instead. She is called Sir David Attenborough, and the sub is called Boaty. Both are very good.",
   "plaque": "antarctic_attenborough",
   "title": "The RRS Sir David Attenborough"
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "THE AIRFIELD. A gravel runway rather than snow or ice, so ordinary wheels can land. Twin Otters on skis or wheels fly scientists and kit to field camps all over the Peninsula, and a larger plane, the Dash 7, flies in from Punta Arenas, in Chile, or from the Falklands. There is no weather forecast so precise as the one on the pilot's face.",
   "plaque": "antarctic_airfield",
   "title": "The Rothera Airfield"
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "MIDWINTER'S DAY. On 21 June, the shortest day in the south, every Antarctic base stops and holds a feast. Scientists dress up, cooks outdo themselves, cards are posted to friends at other bases, and presents are exchanged. It's an Antarctic tradition older than the Treaty, and a very good reason for a pudding.",
   "plaque": "antarctic_midwinter",
   "title": "Midwinter's Day"
  }
 ],
